\title{Description of mizerReef plotting functions}
\description{
In addition to the plotting functions offered by the mizer package,
MizerReef provides new and extended plotting functions to visualize
both input parameters and the results of dynamic simulations for reef
ecosystem models. Several functions adapt or build on mizer's
plotting tools for reef-specific features.
}
\details{
Available plotting functions:

\strong{Input parameter plots}
\tabular{ll}{
Plot \tab Description \cr
\code{\link[=plotRefugeProfile]{plotRefugeProfile()}} \tab Plots the proportion of individuals (by length) that are protected from predators for each species (the refuge profile) \cr
\code{\link[=plotDegradationScale]{plotDegradationScale()}} \tab Plots a heatmap of the degradation scaling for refuge density in degradation simulations across bleaching years and size bins. \cr
\code{\link[=plotVulnerable]{plotVulnerable()}} \tab Plots vulnerability to predation by size and proportion for each species, either at steady state or for a chosen simulation time step. \cr
\code{\link[=plotRefugeDensity]{plotRefugeDensity()}} \tab Plots the density of refuge by size at steady state and through time. \cr
}

\strong{Result plots}
\tabular{ll}{
Plot \tab Description \cr
\code{\link[mizer:plotBiomass]{mizer::plotBiomass()}} \tab Plots the biomass of species and unstructured components through time (mizerReef extends this via a \code{getBiomass.mizerReefSim} method). \cr
\code{\link[mizer:plotSpectra2]{mizer::plotSpectra2()}} \tab Compare two size spectra (e.g., models or scenarios) in one plot. \cr
\code{\link[mizer:plotSpectraRelative]{mizer::plotSpectraRelative()}} \tab Plots relative difference between two spectra. \cr
\code{\link[=plotSpectraPercentChange]{plotSpectraPercentChange()}} \tab Plots percent change between two spectra. \cr
\code{\link[=plotRelativeContribution]{plotRelativeContribution()}} \tab Relative contribution of each species group to total abundance, biomass, and productivity. \cr
\code{\link[=plotProductivity]{plotProductivity()}} \tab Plots productivity for each species through time or at steady state. \cr
\code{\link[=plot2Productivity]{plot2Productivity()}} \tab Productivity of two models or two size ranges in one plot. \cr
\code{\link[=plotProductivityRelative]{plotProductivityRelative()}} \tab Plots the relative or percent change in productivity between two models or scenarios. \cr
\code{\link[=plotTotalAbundance]{plotTotalAbundance()}} \tab Plots total abundance for each species at steady state. \cr
\code{\link[=plotTotalBiomass]{plotTotalBiomass()}} \tab Plots total biomass for each species within a given size range at steady state. \cr
\code{\link[=plot2TotalBiomass]{plot2TotalBiomass()}} \tab Total biomass of two models or two size ranges in one plot. \cr
\code{\link[=plotTotalBiomassRelative]{plotTotalBiomassRelative()}} \tab Relative change in total biomass between two models or scenarios. \cr
}

All plotting functions return a ggplot object, which can be further
customized using the ggplot2 grammar. Most functions accept either a
\link[mizer:MizerSim]{MizerSim} or \link[mizer:MizerParams]{MizerParams} object as input. Species colors and line
types are controlled by the \code{linecolour} and \code{linetype} slots in
\link[mizer:MizerParams]{MizerParams}, and can be changed by the user.

For group or legend naming, you can add a column \code{group_names}
to your \code{species_params} data frame. This column should contain a
nicely formatted character string for each group or species, and will
be used in plot legends and facet labels for improved readability.

Most plots allow selection of a subset of species via the \code{species}
argument. The order of species in legends matches the species parameter
data frame.
}
\section{Usage}{

Call these functions with a \link[mizer:MizerSim]{MizerSim} or \link[mizer:MizerParams]{MizerParams} object containing
simulation results. See individual function documentation for details and
examples.
}

